# Committed readings for p3exit0.wasm and p3panic.wasm, the fork's first GOOS=wasip3 components (#1236, F1).
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run <case>.wasm   (no -W flag: the memory is unshared)
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64, 2026-11-14.
#
# reader                                      reading
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3exit0, 10 of 10        exit 0
# F1's p3panic, wasmtime 49.0.3, 10 of 10     trap unreachable
# Burroughs at d32b0eb on p3exit0, 10 of 10   exit 0
# F1's p3panic, Burroughs at d32b0eb, 10 of 10   trap unreachable
#
# REPLACED by F3a (#1238): p3panic.wasm was rebuilt from the same source on the F3a toolchain, whose runtime
# gives usleep a disposition that returns and lowers stderr, so the panic now prints and exits 2. Its current
# reading is in F3a's section below. The two F1 lines above are kept as the record of what F1's bytes did,
# relabelled so that no test reads them; F1's p3panic.wasm is no longer in this directory.
#
# Meaning:     "exit N" is the process's exit code with a run that ended by wasi:cli/exit@0.3.0's exit;
#              "trap R" is a trap whose kind is R. Neither case wrote a byte to stdout or stderr from the
#              guest: F1 lowers no stdout, so p3panic's message is never printed.
# Burroughs:   `burroughs run <case>.wasm`, a binary built from cmd/burroughs at d32b0eb: p3exit0 exited 0;
#              p3panic exited 4 and printed "burroughs: trap: unreachable" to stderr, 10 of 10 each.
#
# wasmtime's p3panic stderr, identical in all 10 runs (sha256 of each run's stderr compared). The innermost Go
# frame names the refusal: usleep, called by freezetheworld before the panic's first write, which is why the
# trap is usleep's and not write1's.
#
#   Error: failed to run main module `p3panic.wasm`
#
#   Caused by:
#       0: error while executing at wasm backtrace:
#       0: 0x113481 - <unknown>!runtime.abort
#       1:  0x8c788 - <unknown>!runtime.usleep
#       2:  0x9b84a - <unknown>!runtime.freezetheworld
#       3:  0x91743 - <unknown>!runtime.startpanic_m
#       4: 0x101059 - <unknown>!runtime.fatalpanic.func1
#       5: 0x113424 - <unknown>!runtime.systemstack
#       6:  0x91208 - <unknown>!runtime.fatalpanic
#       7: 0x10c157 - <unknown>!runtime.gopanic
#       8: 0x11565a - <unknown>!main.main
#       9:  0x96d10 - <unknown>!runtime.main
#      10: 0x113767 - <unknown>!wasm_pc_f_loop
#      11: 0x113822 - <unknown>!_rt0_wasm_wasip3
#       1: wasm trap: wasm `unreachable` instruction executed
#
# wasmtime exited 134 on each p3panic run.

# Committed readings for p3bind.wasm, F2's witness (#1237): a GOOS=wasip3 guest calling the fork's generated
# WASI 0.3 bindings directly.
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run p3bind.wasm f2-bindings-token   (from this directory)
#              burroughs run p3bind.wasm -- f2-bindings-token
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64, 2026-11-15.
#
# reader                                      reading
# p3bind argv                                 p3bind.wasm f2-bindings-token
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3bind, 10 of 10         exit 127
# Burroughs at 3175bdb on p3bind, 10 of 10    exit 127
#
# Meaning:     the exit code is the bitmask of properties p3bind observed (p3bind.go.txt), reported through
#              wasi:cli/exit@0.3.0's exit-with-code because F2 lowers no stdout. 127 is all seven:
#                0x01 argv[1] is the token            (get-arguments: a list<string> through cabi_realloc)
#                0x02 monotonic now does not decrease  (one flat i64 result, twice)
#                0x04 system-clock now's nanoseconds < 1e9   (the u32 at offset 8 of a 16-byte return area)
#                0x08 its seconds > 1.6e9                    (the s64 at offset 0)
#                0x10 get-random-bytes(16): 1 to 16 bytes, not all zero  (a list<u8> through cabi_realloc)
#                0x20 two get-random-u64 results differ
#                0x40 two get-insecure-seed results are equal  (a tuple<u64, u64> through a return area)
#              Neither engine wrote a byte to stdout or stderr. The argv is the same on both: wasmtime passes
#              the path it was given, and burroughs run passes the file's base name, so run from this
#              directory both are "p3bind.wasm".
#
# The neuters, each run 10 of 10 on both engines, with the builds kept out of this tree:
#   argv[1] "f2-bindings-wrong":                    wasmtime exit 126, Burroughs exit 126 (0x01 clears).
#   instant.nanoseconds read at offset 0, not 8     wasmtime exit 123, Burroughs exit 123 (0x04 clears:
#   (the generated system_clock.go edited):         the low word of the seconds is above 1e9).
#   the linker's trap stand-in forced, internal/wasi's cabi_realloc left unexported: wasmtime exit 134,
#   Burroughs `trap: unreachable` exit 4. wasmtime's stderr, identical in all 10 runs, names the stand-in:
#
#     Error: failed to run main module `p3bind-fallback.wasm`
#
#     Caused by:
#         0: error while executing at wasm backtrace:
#         0: 0x117644 - <unknown>!cabi_realloc
#         1: 0x1c1669 - wit-component:shim!indirect-wasi:cli/environment@0.3.0-get-arguments
#         2: 0x115fb2 - <unknown>!internal_wasi_generated_cli.wasmimportGetArguments
#         3: 0x1163c6 - <unknown>!internal_wasi_generated_cli.GetArguments
#         4: 0x117233 - <unknown>!main.main
#         5:  0x9707c - <unknown>!runtime.main
#         6: 0x113c2b - <unknown>!wasm_pc_f_loop
#         7: 0x113d3e - <unknown>!_rt0_wasm_wasip3
#         1: wasm trap: wasm `unreachable` instruction executed

# Committed readings for p3panic.wasm (rebuilt), p3print.wasm and p3time.wasm, F3a's witnesses (#1238): the fork's
# runtime on real WASI 0.3.0 imports, exit-with-code, both clocks, stdout and stderr over write-via-stream, and a
# usleep that waits rather than traps.
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run <case>.wasm   (from this directory; no flag, no argv)
#              burroughs run <case>.wasm
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64, 2026-11-15.
#
# reader                                        reading
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3panic, 10 of 10          exit 2
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3panic stdout             0 bytes, sha256 e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3panic stderr             69 bytes, sha256 71459a2b8ad68421e1112acb547607b71ba1fce8cf620aa1c1204b0fc02ecd5d
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3print, 10 of 10          exit 3
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3print stdout             71680 bytes, sha256 8b378ad7265e58b1006e6b89366fe0c03ffc6788d0fe5c49919d4e3458986082
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3print stderr             27 bytes, sha256 568a93ade3c233382b21184ba6ab6286945f603d24c18e2d782a2d1eafd83a06
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3time, 10 of 10           exit 7
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3time stdout              0 bytes, sha256 e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3time stderr              0 bytes, sha256 e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855
# Burroughs at cf7fdf6 on each, 10 of 10        the same exit code, stdout and stderr, byte for byte
#
# Meaning:     the exit code is the process's, through wasi:cli/exit@0.3.0's exit-with-code (exit(ok) for 0).
#              Each run's stdout and stderr were hashed, and all 10 wasmtime runs and all 10 Burroughs runs of
#              a case gave one stdout hash and one stderr hash, the ones above.
#   p3panic:   2 is the runtime's fatal-panic code. Its stderr, verbatim (the last line starts with a tab):
#
#     panic: x
#
#     goroutine 1 [running]:
#     main.main()
#     	p3panic/main.go:3 +0x2
#
#   p3print:   stdout is the 70 KiB buffer of p3print.go.txt, written by ONE runtime.write(1, ...); stderr is
#
#     hello
#     wrote 71680 of 71680
#
#   p3time:    7 is every bit: 0x01 monotonic now advanced across a busy loop, 0x02 the wall clock's
#              nanoseconds are below 1e9, 0x04 its seconds are at or past 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z.
#
# Burroughs at bca78b4, before grave #1296's repair (cf7fdf6), printed the same stdout and stderr and then trapped
# at the exit flush's waitable-set.drop, "set 4 still has 1 joined waitable(s)" (2 for p3print): a dropped stream
# or future end stayed in its waitable set. wasmtime dropped the set clean.
#
# Neither engine returned BLOCKED or a partial copy for any write (throwaway builds counting them; a 16 MiB write
# was one copy on both, and on wasmtime also with stdout piped to a slow reader), so write1's wait and its
# partial-copy loop are NOT witnessed by these readings (the fork's phase4/wasip3-f3a.md, "The known gap").
#
# The neuters, each run 10 of 10 on both engines, with the builds kept out of this tree:
#   usleep restored to F1's trap:          p3panic traps before printing anything. wasmtime exit 134, its
#                                          backtrace runtime.abort <- runtime.usleep <- runtime.freezetheworld;
#                                          Burroughs `trap: unreachable`, exit 4. Both stdouts and both
#                                          guest-written stderrs are empty.
#   F1's fictional counter clock restored: p3time exits 3 on both (0x04 clears: the wall clock reads 1970).

# Committed readings for p3c1.wasm, checkpoint C1 (#1241), delivered by F3b (#1295): an ordinary Go program (fmt, os,
# time, crypto/rand; no linkname) whose argv, clocks, sleep, random and environment reach it through the fork's
# wasip3 syscall and os.
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run p3c1.wasm c1-one c1-two   (from this directory; no --env)
#              burroughs run p3c1.wasm -- c1-one c1-two
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64, 2026-11-15.
#
# reader                                        reading
# p3c1 argv                                     p3c1.wasm c1-one c1-two
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3c1, 10 of 10             exit 7
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3c1 stdout                139 bytes, sha256 b4a78277843594e61509403d02d609a8712c1119acb1578c1967e2d19768d041
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3c1 stderr                49 bytes, sha256 5d2983a925ff0e3672b246f3fda15b1481bf8a50453e3ca9a61e84f57a3be702
# Burroughs at 1ed1a87 on p3c1, 10 of 10        the same exit code, stdout and stderr, byte for byte
#
# Meaning:     7 is os.Exit(7), through exit-with-code. All 10 wasmtime runs and all 10 Burroughs runs gave one stdout
#              hash and one stderr hash, the ones above. stdout, verbatim, is every property holding:
#
#     args: [c1-one c1-two]
#     monotonic delta > 0: true
#     year >= 2026: true
#     slept >= 10ms: true
#     crypto/rand not all zero: true
#     getenv X empty: true
#
#              and stderr is the runtime's println and os.Stderr, interleaved through one stream:
#
#     stderr 1: runtime
#     stderr 2: os
#     stderr 3: runtime
#
#              With --env X=hi, wasmtime's getenv line reads false, so the environment is read, not assumed empty.
#
# The neuters, each a temporary edit of the fork, rebuilt, run 10 of 10 on both engines, builds kept out of this tree:
#   argv not pushed (runtimeSetArgsEnvs(nil, ...)): os.Args is empty, so os.Args[1:] panics. Exit 2 on both, no
#                                          stdout, stderr "panic: runtime error: slice bounds out of range [1:0]".
#   RandomGet fills zeros:                 exit 7 on both, the rand line false (140 bytes, sha256 eea34440...).
#   any blocking netpoll traps (F3a's):    the sleep traps after three lines. wasmtime exit 134, its backtrace
#                                          runtime.abort <- netpollBlockRefused <- netpoll <- findRunnable;
#                                          Burroughs `trap: unreachable`, exit 4.
#   netpoll(delay) returns at once:        SURVIVES, byte-identical, exit 7. findRunnable spins to the deadline, so
#                                          the sleep still lasts 10 ms; only host CPU differs (a throwaway 1 s sleep:
#                                          Burroughs 0.07 s user with the wait, 1.12 s with the neuter).
#   fds 1 and 2 through a second stream    SURVIVES, byte-identical, exit 7. Both engines write each COMPLETED copy
#   each, opened in syscall.Write and      through at once and lose nothing left unflushed at exit, F3a's known gap
#   never flushed:                         again: one stream per fd is built, not witnessed (the fork's
#                                          phase4/wasip3-f3b.md).

# Committed readings for p3poll.wasm and p3stdin.wasm, F5's witnesses (#1240): the fork's single-M netpoller over one
# waitable set, so a goroutine blocks alone; os.Pipe as a guest-to-guest stream<u8>; and os.Stdin read through it.
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run p3poll.wasm < /dev/null        (from this directory; no flag)
#              .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run p3stdin.wasm < ../p3cat.stdin  (and < /dev/null)
#              burroughs run <case>.wasm, with the same stdin
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64, 2026-11-15.
#
# reader                                                  reading
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3poll, 10 of 10                     exit 0
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3poll stdout                        312 bytes, sha256 613ea0b8c6bffa7dde211e824d1b738f97f566b5d97c87f74189b09269fed8df
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3poll stderr                        0 bytes, sha256 e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3stdin < p3cat.stdin, 10 of 10      exit 0
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3stdin < p3cat.stdin stdout         144 bytes, sha256 4be28a9625ac0a0126378663245926f1b0716e3c4a78a5f9861907401ad0e62b
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3stdin < p3cat.stdin stderr         0 bytes, sha256 e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3stdin < /dev/null, 10 of 10        exit 0
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3stdin < /dev/null stdout           72 bytes, sha256 136a033d3d0f6aaa9c9ce4da74188b700172b0a0564a7fc14df3076b1eaa0bbc
# wasmtime 49.0.3 on p3stdin < /dev/null stderr           0 bytes, sha256 e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855
# Burroughs at 954c0b1 on each, 10 of 10                  the same exit code, stdout and stderr, byte for byte
#
# Meaning:     all 10 wasmtime runs and all 10 Burroughs runs of each row gave one stdout hash and one stderr hash, the
#              ones above. p3poll's stdout, verbatim, is every property holding and one order:
#
#     A got ping: true
#     B ran while A was parked: true
#     slept >= 10ms: true
#     A read pong: true
#     B's write returned after A read: true
#     A read EOF after B closed: true
#     readers ran: 2:two 1:one
#     read timed out: true
#     next read got the late bytes: true
#     closed a read end mid-read: true
#     write after the reader closed fails: true
#
#              "readers ran: 2:two 1:one" is the netpoller's order: both readers' events are ready together, one
#              waitable-set.poll drain takes reader 1's and then reader 2's, and the runtime runs the last readied
#              first. p3stdin's stdout on ../p3cat.stdin (20770 bytes) is p3cat's on the same bytes (../p3cat.reading),
#              with p3stdin's name:
#
#     p3stdin: read 20770 bytes, checksum c1c68b27, 310 lines
#     p3stdin: first line: p3cat stdin line 0001: the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog
#
#              and on /dev/null "p3stdin: read 0 bytes, checksum 00000000, 0 lines" and "p3stdin: first line: " (the
#              line is empty, the colon followed by one space).
#
# The neuters, each a temporary edit of the fork, rebuilt, run on both engines (10 of 10, or 3 of 3 where the run
# hangs, under a 10 s timeout), builds kept out of this tree:
#   netpoll returns at once with nothing:  p3poll and p3stdin hang on both, with no stdout (timeout, exit 124).
#   the waitable-set.poll drain removed:   p3poll exit 0 on both, its order line "readers ran: 1:one 2:two" (312 bytes,
#                                          sha256 5a0125ec...): each wait readies one reader, in the set's order.
#                                          p3stdin SURVIVES, byte-identical: its events come one at a time.
#   an unbounded netpoll traps (F3b's):    p3stdin traps at its first read on both stdins. wasmtime exit 134, its
#                                          backtrace runtime.abort <- runtime.netpoll <- runtime.findRunnable;
#                                          Burroughs `trap: unreachable`, exit 4. p3poll SURVIVES, byte-identical:
#                                          every wait it makes has a timer or finds its event already ready.
#   stdin's Read answers EBADF (F3b's):    p3stdin exit 1 on both stdins and both engines, stderr "p3stdin: reading stdin
#                                          failed: read /dev/stdin: Bad file number".
#   stdin reports no FDFLAG_NONBLOCK:      os.Stdin is not registered with the poller, so EAGAIN reaches the program:
#                                          exit 1 on both, "read /dev/stdin: Try again".
#   Close drops a read end mid-read        p3poll's sixth phase traps at the drop on both. wasmtime exit 1, "cannot
#   without cancelling it:                 remove busy stream" in syscall.(*streamFD).close; Burroughs exit 4,
#                                          "stream.drop-readable: end 17 is mid-copy (COPYING)".
#   netpoll's timer cancelled while still  p3stdin traps at its second read on both. wasmtime exit 134 in
#   joined to the set:                     runtime.wasip3SubtaskCancel; Burroughs exit 4, "subtask.cancel: subtask 4 is
#                                          joined to a waitable set".
#   a Write's retry not recognised:        the parked "pong" write is issued twice. p3poll stops after its four
#                                          first lines on both: wasmtime blocks in its waitable-set.wait until the
#                                          timeout (exit 124); the burroughs process exits 2 with Go's "all goroutines
#                                          are asleep - deadlock!" and its own goroutine dump, goroutine 1 parked in
#                                          the guest's waitable-set.wait (waitableSetWait).
#   Close's release leaves a cancelled     wasmtime traps at the sync stream.cancel-read, exit 134, "waitable cannot be
#   read end joined to the set:            used synchronously while added to a waitable set", as the pin does
#                                          (def:cancel_copy). Burroughs at 954c0b1 does NOT trap: exit 0, p3poll's
#                                          reading byte for byte. Its stream.cancel-read has no in-a-waitable-set check.
#                                          Found here, reported to the owner, not repaired in this slice.
